    <node id="Tiny Tim">
      <data key="d0">Tiny Tim</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Christmas Carol by Charles Dickens features Tiny Tim, the youngest son of Bob and Mrs. Cratchit, as a central character. He suffers from a debilitating illness that has left him unable to walk or play with other children. The extent of his physical disability varies across descriptions: some portray him using a crutch for mobility, while others describe him requiring assistance due to a withered leg.

Despite these variations, the overall narrative portrays Tiny Tim as a kind and gentle soul who brings hope and joy to those around him. His death has a profound impact on the characters in the story, particularly his family members. However, it's worth noting that one description suggests an alternate outcome where Tiny Tim does not die but rather becomes a second father figure.

Throughout the novel, Tiny Tim is depicted as having a beautiful singing voice and a strong spirit despite his physical limitations. His character serves as a catalyst for Ebenezer Scrooge's transformation from a miserly businessman to a kinder and more compassionate individual. The Cratchit family's love and concern for Tiny Tim are evident in their efforts to care for him, and his presence highlights the importance of kindness, generosity, and compassion during the holiday season.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5eeebd4a7c0b1417f8eb4d15431f546d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff9deb2924e688bbce0e0754c1c6c61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f65a52cdced73f0bcc13216f1ce39e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df28678965f69c7e76c314f07ac980b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17a39495d7b79a72917d7f65a38a5e46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-46e6c7e69332f5941d7559d38ab18692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1396694e41876e83b97376904aee5073&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-118ca942f4f3c9502142773f3fdd20d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d515d836ef3c169d415fdd151180e25&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b8276083af810245d6150ef51adb0c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b1f20d45706bc18474add4f6ec6ab5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffd1a7ae5fb09fc2adae0d7ed19d8e16</data>
